WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:06.410
The greatest public speakers became great
because they eventually found their voice.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:09.179
Not somebody else's voice, but their voice.

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:11.170
Let me explain to you what I mean by that.

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:19.960
Have you ever sat in a room where somebody
is speaking and it's so awkward or uncomfortable

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.480
that you truly cannot wait to leave the room.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.610
Like you are going lower in your chair and
you're trying to hide and maybe putting the

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.610
hat down.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.990
You just don't want to be seen, and you're
feeling sorry for the speaker.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:30.990
Why is that?

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:32.090
Here's why.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:38.620
It's because the person is trying to speak
like somebody else, and they're not that person,

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:40.089
they're not like the other person.

00:00:40.089 --> 00:00:43.329
The best way to give an analogy that will
make sense to you is this.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:44.329
Think about music.

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:46.679
How many different music genres are there?

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:48.389
Think about all the different music genres
that we have.

00:00:48.389 --> 00:00:50.409
Now I want you to visualize this.

00:00:50.409 --> 00:00:52.769
Take Tupac.

00:00:52.769 --> 00:00:56.469
Imagine Tupac singing country music.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:00:58.569
Can you see it?

00:00:58.569 --> 00:01:00.249
I can't see it.

00:01:00.249 --> 00:01:05.640
Like imagine he's [singing country style].

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.010
I can't even do it right now.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.390
[Singing].

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.490
It's his song.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.430
Now take Blake Shelton.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.340
Now imagine Blake Shelton, we ask him to sing
hip hop.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.490
Can you see it?

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.220
Can you truly see it?

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.640
Can you see Blake Shelton singing hip hop?

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.000
I can't see it.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.430
Because that's not his voice.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.210
And that's not Tupac's voice.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.120
And you need to find your voice.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.149
And when people ask me this most random question,
"Pat, I want to be a better public speaker.

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:34.229
What can I do?"

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:35.590
Find your voice!

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.579
Stop trying to be somebody else's voice.

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:38.579
What does that even mean?

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:43.640
So finally, instead of answering hundreds
of emails that I get on this, I decided to

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:44.640
make video again.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.320
Keep in mind, every single video I make, I
make because the question gets asked so many

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.700
times I eventually make a video about it.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:50.929
So let's get right into it.

00:01:50.929 --> 00:01:56.280
I've got some points that I'll give you about
speaking, 16 of them, but before getting into

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:02.159
it, I first want to tell you multiple styles
of public speakers, and you need to ask yourself

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:06.000
which one is you and identify with that speaker
and then go study speakers that have that

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.550
style because that's your style, if that makes
sense.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.479
So let's get right into it.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.780
#1: Mentor/Advisor

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.670
So who is the mentor/advisor?

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.040
This could be a Tony Robbins type of a speaker.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.750
When he speaks, he can't help himself but
give you advice.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.120
He can't help himself but give you direction.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.620
He can't help himself but lead you, right?

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.410
Here's what I would do.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:28.670
. . because that's their style.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:32.879
The mentor/advisor, high, high energy, that's
a Tony Robbins.

00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:34.700
#2: Charm/Humor

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.890
This could be a Ronald Reagan.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:41.250
When he would speak, I can't think of a time
when he spoke where he didn't have a joke

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.250
to say.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:45.540
It was always humor, charm, smile.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.710
His pacing was slow.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.530
He wasn't the fast talking guy.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:53.720
Tony Robbins can run laps around Ronald Reagan
when it comes to speaking, because Tony can

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:58.819
say a thousand words in 30 seconds and Reagan
is just on number 68, for instance.

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:00.560
I'm giving you an example here.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.410
So charm/humor may be a Ronald Reagan, with
a sense of humor.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.050
You may say, "I have a great sense of humor."

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.050
Use it.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.299
"People have told me I'm charming."

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:08.299
Use it.

00:03:08.299 --> 00:03:09.299
Go study Ronald Reagan.

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:10.299
#3:Bold, Yet Credible

00:03:10.299 --> 00:03:14.329
Somebody may say, "Well, I'm more like bold,
you know, I just kind of want to tell people

00:03:14.329 --> 00:03:16.840
off, but I know what I'm talking about."

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.939
Well then that's Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:03:18.939 --> 00:03:19.939
He's extremely bold.

00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:24.220
He'll get up and curse the entire audience
out, but he knows what he's talking about.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.459
So bold, yet credible.

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:27.730
Some people say, "I don't like Gary's style."

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.270
He's not talking to you!

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.569
He's got his own style.

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:32.469
But he's bold, yet credible.

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:34.690
Some people don't like Reagan.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.459
I know people that say, "I can't even listen
to Tony Robbins' voice.

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:40.230
I can't listen to Tony Robbins."

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:41.959
He's not speaking to you.

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:44.000
You're not the audience.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.629
#4: Locker Room Talk

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:46.629
You may say, "You know what?

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:48.870
I'm more like the locker room type of guy.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.790
I like to sit down and tell people what they
need to be doing."

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:52.790
Great.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:53.959
You may study Mike Ditka.

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:56.629
Mike Ditka was a locker room talk type of
a guy.

00:03:56.629 --> 00:03:59.610
He sat there and told people, "Here's what
you need to do."

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:03.739
And he was locker room, fired everybody up,
ticked them off and boom!

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:04.900
They're ready to go to war.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:05.900
That's Mike Ditka.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.030
Go study Mike Ditka, the coach of the Chicago
Bears.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:12.620
A lot of people don't like him, but he did
very well as a player and as a football coach.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:13.620
#4:

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:18.200
Another one may be commanding, firm, generalesque,
like Netanyahu.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:25.800
Prime Minister Netanyahu from Israel just
recently gave a talk to the United Nations,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:26.800
I believe.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:35.410
He gave a talk that I recommend you listen
to because he was firm, slow, bold, he respected

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:39.060
everybody, opened it up with respect to everybody,
but he made his point.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.570
Commanding, firm, generalesque.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:41.670
That may be you.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.490
Many people don't like his style, but you
may speak like that.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:45.660
You need to be true to your voice.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.660
#6: Crusade

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:47.660
The next one is crusade.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.030
You may be a crusader, and nobody really understands.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:50.730
What do you mean you're a crusader?

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.390
Why do you always talk about correcting an
injustice?

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:58.440
Maybe you need to study MLK, Billy Graham,
Malcolm X.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.500
By the way, conflicting beliefs.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.470
Malcolm X and MLK and Billy Graham, they're
not the same beliefs, but they had a crusade.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:07.490
It was a cause, right?

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:11.060
So that's the category I would be studying
if that's your style of speaking.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.530
You may say, "That's not me."

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:14.600
Then keep continuing on the next people.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:15.600
#7: Storyteller

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:16.600
Imagination.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:17.600
Steve Jobs.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:24.620
Steve Jobs does not speak like Ronald Reagan
or speak like Tony Robbins or Gary or any

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:25.620
of these other guys.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.360
But when Steve Jobs spoke, people listened
because he was an incredible storyteller and

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.560
he was an unbelievable guy at making you and
I imagine things that weren't possible.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.870
Can you imagine if you and I. . . the Walkman,
and all of a sudden he takes out the iPod.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.270
Oh my gosh!

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.670
It's an iPod!

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:45.990
Can you imagine having a computer within your
hands and he takes out an iPad.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.250
Do you remember the first time you and I found
out about iPads?

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:49.250
Everybody wanted one.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:50.770
Remember the first time somebody had an iPod?

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:51.920
How did you and I react?

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.670
How many songs?

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:54.840
5,000 songs!

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.000
That's like me having 5,000 CDs.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.000
Imagination.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:03.540
Can you imagine if you have an iPod, iPod
Touch, iPad all together, called the iPhone!

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.480
I mean, it's imagination.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.780
So maybe your gift is imagination.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.490
#9: Presence

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:10.520
What's presence?

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:11.720
Oprah Winfrey.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.460
When she enters a room, presence entered.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:15.830
There's presence.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:18.730
So her speaking style may be presence.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:20.410
It may be curiosity.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:22.440
It may be questions that she does.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:23.550
But that's her style.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:24.550
#10: Arrogant

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:25.910
You know what arrogant is?

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:26.910
This is not who you want to be.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:30.400
This is somebody that acts like they know
what they're talking about, yet everybody's

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.890
