00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:57.649
side to speaking.

00:10:57.649 --> 00:10:58.649
How big is the stage?

00:10:58.649 --> 00:10:59.649
Do you walk?

00:10:59.649 --> 00:11:00.649
It there a podium?

00:11:00.649 --> 00:11:01.649
Is the mic over here?

00:11:01.649 --> 00:11:02.649
Do you hold the mic?

00:11:02.649 --> 00:11:04.089
Is it a wireless mic?

00:11:04.089 --> 00:11:06.139
Is is tied here?

00:11:06.139 --> 00:11:07.139
Can you walk around?

00:11:07.139 --> 00:11:09.839
Do you need to make sure the speakers are
on this side so you don't make that sound

00:11:09.839 --> 00:11:10.839
[microphone squeal].

00:11:10.839 --> 00:11:12.519
You need to know your real estate.

00:11:12.519 --> 00:11:15.660
There are things that you need to know, but
that's technical stuff.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:16.959
Forget about the technical stuff.

00:11:16.959 --> 00:11:20.170
Technical stuff happens after you've given
thousands of talks, you'll realize about the

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.360
different AV systems, lighting, where you
need to stand to have the lighting perfect,

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:24.360
and your face.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:25.360
That's purely technical.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:26.499
I'm giving you basic how-to.

00:11:26.499 --> 00:11:27.950
I'm not getting technical here.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:31.380
I'm giving you basic how-to when it comes
down to speaking, and hopefully some of these

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:32.910
things can help you out.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:37.089
Listen, for me, when I think about a great
public speaker, this is my #1.

00:11:37.089 --> 00:11:40.300
Let me tell you what my #1 is.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:46.060
If I can go and hear somebody speak - whoever
it is - any one of these guys.

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:56.029
If I can go and hear somebody speak, and the
entire time, when this person is speaking,

00:11:56.029 --> 00:12:01.709
if I go hear somebody speak, and I feel like,
the audience could have thousands of people

00:12:01.709 --> 00:12:05.390
in the room, but I feel like it's just me
and the person speaking?

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:08.069
That to me is a great speaker.

00:12:08.069 --> 00:12:11.790
If it's just you and I. That to me is a great
speaker.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:19.559
If I get lost during his speech, and I forget
everybody else is in the room, I forget I'm

00:12:19.559 --> 00:12:21.449
watching a video.

00:12:21.449 --> 00:12:23.930
I forget I'm watching a movie.

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:30.430
I'm watching, like for instance, when Gladiator,
when Russell Crowe gives that one speech,

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:34.839
no one exists and you're just in the movie,
you are there.

00:12:34.839 --> 00:12:47.620
Or when Braveheart, what's his name, Mel Gibson
gets up and he gives that one speech, you

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:48.709
watch that.

00:12:48.709 --> 00:12:50.810
No one exists.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:51.810
Right?

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:58.279
Or you're Denzel Washington give a talk in
John Q, I'm not going to bury my son.

00:12:58.279 --> 00:13:00.059
My son's going to bury who?

00:13:00.059 --> 00:13:01.059
Me.

00:13:01.059 --> 00:13:02.819
You're lost.

00:13:02.819 --> 00:13:03.980
It's one person.

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:08.480
If a speaker knows how to do that, they all
do it in their own separate ways.

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:11.400
That to me is a great speaker.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:13.844
So if you can captivate me to the point where
I think it's just you and I, as if you and

00:13:13.844 --> 00:13:18.190
I are having a cup of coffee or we're having
lunch and I'm simply listening to you speak,

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:19.190
you've already won.

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:21.010
You don't even need to watch the rest of the
video.

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:22.999
Turn off the video and skip to another video.

00:13:22.999 --> 00:13:24.850
You don't need to watch anything else.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:27.260
If you learn how to do that, the rest is over
with.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:29.189
The rest is over with.

00:13:29.189 --> 00:13:32.161
Because all of the other stuff, you'll pick
up by doing so many times of speaking, you'll

00:13:32.161 --> 00:13:33.161
get better at it.

00:13:33.161 --> 00:13:34.499
Next thing.

00:13:34.499 --> 00:13:42.309
#2 to me is somebody that is very good at
making their point by telling stories.

00:13:42.309 --> 00:13:46.800
Sometimes when I get asked to speak for three
hours and they want to do Q&amp;A, that's a different

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:51.740
talk than when it's for an hour, to just give
a presentation than it is from hey, can you

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:53.040
give a 10-minute talk here?

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:54.369
Then a 5-minute talk.

00:13:54.369 --> 00:13:59.119
If I'm asked to talk 5 minutes on TV or on
whatever it is that I'm speaking for five

00:13:59.119 --> 00:14:01.619
minutes, here's a point, here's a story.

00:14:01.619 --> 00:14:02.619
Done.

00:14:02.619 --> 00:14:05.119
Let me tell you about what's going on over
here.

00:14:05.119 --> 00:14:08.269
Here's the conditions that we're looking at
right now.

00:14:08.269 --> 00:14:10.470
. . . Here's what I think we need to consider
changing to.

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:12.240
And here's what I believe will be the solution.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.660
Let me tell you why I believe this.

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:17.920
I remember when it was 1992 and boom.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:18.920
Story.

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:22.629
So get into your story, when you wanted to
make your point, tell stories.

00:14:22.629 --> 00:14:26.670
Somebody who is very good at making stories
and figuring out ways for me to take a metaphor

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:30.499
or an analogy, that makes sense, I got it.

00:14:30.499 --> 00:14:35.000
I respond to that, and most people respond
to that as well if you're telling your story.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:36.819
#3:Make Your Case

00:14:36.819 --> 00:14:39.879
So look at speaking as these three different
points.

00:14:39.879 --> 00:14:42.930
Write these three points down so you can kind
of understand what I'm saying with this.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.610
First, state your facts.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:53.110
For instance, "Unemployment right now in America
is 9.2%" (It's not, I'm just making it up.)

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:56.939
"Here's what I think we need to do.

00:14:56.939 --> 00:15:00.149
We need to get people going back to jobs by
lowering the taxes."

00:15:00.149 --> 00:15:06.420
Or "we need to raise the taxes and tax the
top 1% and make them pay the bottom 80%.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:07.699
That's what we need to do.

00:15:07.699 --> 00:15:12.430
And if we do this, I believe unemployment
is going to go from 9.2% to 4.3%."

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:18.610
Take that three format -- state the facts,
make your argument, why they should join you.

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.420
State your facts, make your argument, why
they should join you.

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:22.420
Just follow that format.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:27.441
And by the way, practice this and with any
argument, it doesn't matter what it is, take

00:15:27.441 --> 00:15:30.699
anything, follow those three steps, and look
at what will happen.

00:15:30.699 --> 00:15:34.879
Then you'll get judged based on how well you
do in the middle.

00:15:34.879 --> 00:15:35.879
Think about it.

00:15:35.879 --> 00:15:38.480
This is when you speak like that and you're
an attorney, you're going to get judged based

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:39.480
on the middle.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:41.560
By the way, this could happen with family
as well.

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:43.309
You're sitting there saying, "Listen.

00:15:43.309 --> 00:15:44.619
Let's state the facts.

00:15:44.619 --> 00:15:49.010
Let's say you're talking to your kid.

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:50.990
Here's your grades.

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:53.639
And let me explain to you what I think is
taking place.

00:15:53.639 --> 00:15:56.911
I think one of the reasons is because you're
doing this, this, this, and I think if you

00:15:56.911 --> 00:15:59.019
do this, here's what could happen.

00:15:59.019 --> 00:16:01.981
And you always wanted to play baseball for
Pepperdine University because that's the way

00:16:01.981 --> 00:16:05.160
you want to go into major league baseball,
this is your opportunity.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:09.389
So I think if you do this, here's a fact,
we can get some scholarships, you can go play

00:16:09.389 --> 00:16:11.550
for Pepperdine and maybe one day you'll play
major league baseball.

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:12.550
What do you say?

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:13.550
What do you think about that?

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:14.550
Three step process.

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:15.550
Those three.

00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:19.279
State your facts, make your argument, and
why they should join you, and if they do what's

00:16:19.279 --> 00:16:21.470
going to happen.

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:26.839
So you're speaking like an attorney because
you're making a case.

00:16:26.839 --> 00:16:31.699
#4: If you are a first time speaker, or you've
only spoken a handful of times, typically

00:16:31.699 --> 00:16:37.220
things that will make you sound like you know
what you're doing, you've done this a few

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:39.589
times before, let's just say you got three
points you're making.

00:16:39.589 --> 00:16:42.499
To each point, add a quote to it.

00:16:42.499 --> 00:16:46.350
Winston Churchill once said, history will
be kind to me for I intend to write it.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:52.010
I think we need to focus on writing our own
history by writing our goals and commitments

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:53.749
on what we want to do.

00:16:53.749 --> 00:16:56.029
Okay, great.

00:16:56.029 --> 00:17:00.569
"Imagination is more important than knowledge"
was said by Einstein.

00:17:00.569 --> 00:17:03.920
I think sometimes we spend too much time on
the facts and today we need to spend some

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:05.720
time on our dreams.

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:07.549
That's your point #2, dreams.

00:17:07.549 --> 00:17:13.120
But point, quote, point, quote, point, quote,
especially if you're newer.

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:16.880
The more you speak, the more you read, the
more quotes you have in your repertoire, they're

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:21.190
going to come out, just purely by you having
done these talks so many times.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:24.180
So add a quote, it will make it a lot easier
for you.

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:25.370
#5: Connect with the Audience

00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:27.060
How do you connect with the audience?

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.340
Look at them, and make eye contact with them.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:31.770
Make eye contact with them.

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:36.280
And choose a handful of people that are engaged
the most, and make eye contact with them.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:37.290
Don't worry about the guy yawning.

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:38.840
Don't even look at the person that's yawning.

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:40.490
They're going to confuse you.

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:43.640
Look at the person that is fully engaged,
and make eye contact.

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.840
Keep going back, boom, boom, boom, boom, boom.

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:48.440
Boom, boom, boom, boom.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.190
Especially when you're newer.

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:53.560
Make eye contact, and by the way, one thing
you don't want to do is just stay on one person.

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:57.140
A lot of times amateurs stay on one person,
and that person is very uncomfortable and

00:17:57.140 --> 00:17:59.620
everybody else is saying, hey, do you want
to talk to us as well?

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:05.390
So you have to make sure you're dancing [looking
side to side], dancing with everybody.

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:07.730
Eye contact everywhere.

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:13.830
#6: Next is I said it already, don't try to
sound like others.

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:17.120
Pick your voice, and stick to that voice and
who you are.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:23.580
You can many times ask somebody on your style
of speaking and if you made a list of these,

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:26.290
and you put them out there, someone's going
to say, You know what, I see you more as the

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:27.500
this type of a speaker.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:28.500
Great.

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:32.290
Then take that and ask yourself which one
you are, and work on that type of a speaker.

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:35.140
But make sure you're staying true to your
own voice.

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:36.510
#7: Preparation

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:38.180
Simple as that.

00:18:38.180 --> 00:18:43.140
When I first started speaking initially and
I was very nervous about speaking.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:51.730
The first time I spoke, I spoke at Denny's
in Compton.

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:56.800
Out of all the places, I spoke at a Denny's
in Compton.

00:18:56.800 --> 00:19:01.350
And it was last minute, the speaker just asked
me to come up and I was not prepared to speak.

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:02.680
I had never spoken in my life.

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:05.080
I'm a military guy, but he asked me to come
up.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:07.400
And I went up, and I couldn't wait to sit
down.

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:09.160
It was the worst talk I ever gave in my life.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:11.130
I was nervous like you wouldn't believe.

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:14.710
And then from there I started doing more of
them and then eventually there was a system

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:15.710
to it.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:20.410
But I realized if I'm not a pro, because I
haven't given thousands of talks in my life,

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:23.110
I need to make sure I over prepare.

00:19:23.110 --> 00:19:26.650
So I focused on preparation.

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:29.900
The easiest way, sometimes people try to write
their entire speech, that's okay.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:33.430
I don't recommend it because I don't listen
when somebody writes their entire speech.

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:37.710
So I'm telling you as a listener, I will not
listen to your speech if you just write the

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:39.742
whole script, and you're going through it.

00:19:39.742 --> 00:19:43.840
You're going to have a hard time getting my
attention if you're just you know, people

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:46.820
will do one of these things they'll say, you
know, I'm so nervous I'm just going to read

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:47.820
my speech.

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:50.550
Okay so, "Hi. . .[reading in monotone]."

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:55.880
You lost me already.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:56.980
I'm gone.

00:19:56.980 --> 00:20:02.680
So I suggest taking a card, make five bullet
points, and under each bullet point make three

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:05.560
points if you need to make it and add a story
to it.

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:09.420
You may put something like "Volkswagen story"
and you know what the Volkswagen story is,

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:12.110
so get into it.

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:15.050
Hugo Boss story.

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:18.120
This is a suit one time you had and it ripped
and you went on to the appointment and they

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:21.760
laughed at you and you realized before they
even laughed at you that you were going to

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:25.650
tell them, I'm so sorry, when I was coming
in, I hit the door and I cut my pants, so

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:28.280
my apologies for these pants and I'll make
sure I get it fixed before the next time we

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:29.280
meet together.

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:30.280
Haha.

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:31.280
Let's get into the meeting.

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:32.280
For instance.

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:33.280
But that's your story, okay?

00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:35.120
So the more prepared you are as a rookie,
the better it is.

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:40.040
Even as a veteran, but as a rookie, even more
important.

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:42.060
#8: What is your outcome?

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:44.060
What is truly your outcome for giving the
talk?

00:20:44.060 --> 00:20:48.420
What is your ideal outcome of what you want
to start seeing happen with this talk?

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:49.930
What is the action item?

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:51.930
What do you want them to talk action with?

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:52.930
Truly.

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:53.930
Is it to buy?

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:55.210
Is it to register?

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:56.450
Is it to sign up?

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:57.790
Is it to subscribe?

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:58.790
Is it to move?

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:00.050
It is to be somewhere else?

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:01.050
Is it to.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:02.050
. . so what is your outcome?

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:04.830
What is truly your outcome of this talk you're
giving?

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:09.350
Be very specific of your outcome and make
sure you're staying true to your outcome.

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:11.700
Sometimes you're giving so much, like I'm
doing such a good job!

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:15.340
And then you finish and you're like, I didn't
even hit my outcome!

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:17.570
What is your outcome?

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:20.380
#9: Know your audience

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:22.370
It's very important to know who you're speaking
to.

00:21:22.370 --> 00:21:23.930
So, how old are they?

00:21:23.930 --> 00:21:24.930
Income?

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:26.790
Where are they from?

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:28.470
Why are they there to hear you?

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:29.530
Do they care to hear you?

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:30.530
Do they like you?

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:32.800
Do they even know who you are?

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:33.830
Do they pay attention?

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:35.140
Are they younger?

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:38.380
If they're younger, how much of an attention
span do they have?

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:39.930
I've spoken in front of prisons.

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:43.630
I've spoken in front of people who just got
out of prison.

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:45.000
I've spoken in front of billionaires.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:46.280
I've spoken in front of millionaires.

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:47.630
I've spoken in front of clergy.

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:49.950
I've spoken in front of atheists.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:52.020
I've spoken in front of a debate politically.

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:54.580
So who is your audience that you're speaking
to?

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:58.030
Then you'll know how to make the adjustments
based on the audience you're speaking to.

00:21:58.030 --> 00:21:59.150
#10: Action Items

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:02.500
Put your action items, similar to your outcome,
but action items.

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:06.390
My action items are first I want them to do
this, two I want them to do that, three I

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:07.390
would like them to do this.

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:08.720
So 10, give your action items.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:10.500
Point #1, here's what I want you to do.

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:14.570
I want you to go and do . . . go to the website
and register.

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:19.150
#2, I need you to send me an email, right
now, take out your phone and send me an email

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:23.060
and tell me what is the thing that you're
going to be changing about yourself today?

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:24.060
Whatever it is.

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:28.150
And 3, the pamphlet I have underneath you,
when you're leaving, I need you to come to

00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:30.970
my booth, when you're leaving, I need you
to go buy this book.

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:31.970
It doesn't matter.

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:34.560
Give your action items when you're leaving.

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:39.070
#11: typically it's good to come and meet
the audience before, shake hands, greet, talk

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:41.350
to people, let them get to know you, you get
to know them.

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:43.840
There's some kind of a relationship going
on, and when you're speaking from stage you

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:47.870
can typically identify one of the people you
spoke with and that helps out a lot when you're

00:22:47.870 --> 00:22:48.870
speaking.

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:52.150
#12: Edify the venue and the organization
that brought you up.

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:56.430
For instance, if you're speaking somewhere
and they ask you to speak, don't right off

00:22:56.430 --> 00:23:02.290
the bat go and say thank you so much for this
-- you're too black and white.

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:06.200
Get into it and break the ice and talk and
then five minutes into it say hey, by the

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:11.150
way, thank you for the invite, but make sure
you're recognizing the people that gave you

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:14.231
that platform because without that platform,
you wouldn't have the platform and the audience

00:23:14.231 --> 00:23:15.920
you're speaking to.

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:18.780
#13: Disturb

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:21.620
The greatest speakers I know know how to disturb
and move people.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:23.540
These guys [fear] are good at disturbing.

00:23:23.540 --> 00:23:27.630
Great leaders are phenomenal at disturbing
and getting people to move.

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:32.290
You better believe crusade speakers are very
much about disturbing.

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:34.640
Bold yet credible are all about disturbing.

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:36.330
Locker room talk are about disturbing.

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:38.180
Commanding, they're about disturbing.

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:42.160
So figure out a way how to disturb and make
me move.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:45.560
So think about people that spoke to you, how
did it make you move?

00:23:45.560 --> 00:23:47.840
You be that person to your audience.

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:49.780
#14: Challenge them

00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:50.780
There's got to be a challenge.

00:23:50.780 --> 00:23:52.120
I have a challenge for you.

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:53.120
Here's my challenge to you.

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:54.640
My challenge to you is to do this, this, this.

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:57.190
So there's got to be a challenge given to
everybody.

00:23:57.190 --> 00:24:00.990
The greatest talk ever you give, without a
challenge at the end, is worthless.

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:02.210
Just so you know.

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:06.020
If I don't leave after hearing you speak and
I don't take any action and don't have a challenge

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:08.430
I need to be doing, your talk was a waste
of time.

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:09.710
Within an hour I'm over it already.

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:10.860
No one's talking about it.

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:12.700
But you need to give me a challenge.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:16.480
The best type of speakers are the ones that
I walk away from saying, "Man, I feel like

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:17.480
I'm not good enough.

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:18.480
I've got to improve on this.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:19.480
I've got to do this."

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:20.480
Challenge.

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:21.480
I have a challenge.

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:23.670
What is your challenge you're going to give
to your audience?

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:25.590
#15: Rehearse

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:29.050
Practice your talk, especially if you're newer,
in front of other people.

00:24:29.050 --> 00:24:31.220
#16: Get Your Timing Right

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:32.220
What is timing?

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:33.710
When you put your story here.

00:24:33.710 --> 00:24:34.710
When you disturb.

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:36.510
When you do this.

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:38.800
Sports is all about timing.

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:40.870
This [shooting a basket] is timing.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:41.920
Football is timing.

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:43.250
Golf is timing.

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:44.790
This is a game of timing.

00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:45.790
Speaking is also timing.

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:49.320
And by the way, with timing, timing is probably
the last thing you're going to pick up because

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.000
it's not going to come right off the bat.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:53.570
You won't get timing until you give 100, 200,
300 talks.

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:56.990
So just something to be thinking about and
the more you give, you'll know what I mean

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:57.990
by timing.

00:24:57.990 --> 00:25:01.450
Comment on the bottom and I'll do my best
to get back to you with the comments, but

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:02.540
timing comes later.

00:25:02.540 --> 00:25:03.600
That doesn't come early on.

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:05.070
Those are some points I have for you.

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:06.070
How many of them was it?

00:25:06.070 --> 00:25:09.910
Was it 16 points I got for you on how to become
a better public speaker.

00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:13.600
But you may want to go back and look at these
and pick and choose who it is and start studying

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:19.770
that person's style of speaking and mainly
focus on that person's style of speaking.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:25.050
Don't try to be somebody you're not, and you'll
be amazed by the type of talks you'll eventually

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:26.050
start giving.

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:27.340
And by the way, keep this in mind.

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:32.230
It helps to give a lot of talks so this is
one of the benefits of being an entrepreneur

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:36.990
and being in sales you have many different
opportunities to give talks to your sales

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:37.990
organization.

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:40.870
Sometimes people who are involved in sales
end up becoming great speakers because they

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:43.730
have more opportunities to give talks than
somebody who is not.

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:49.890
So I highly recommend being involved in some
type of a sales organization, sales model,

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:53.010
where you can constantly be doing this to
see the reaction you're getting, the response

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:54.660
